          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which histological pattern in the cervical stroma is virtually pathognomonic for</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which histological pattern in the cervical stroma is virtually pathognomonic for Chlamydia trachomatis infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neutrophilic infiltrate with microabscesses</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A neutrophilic infiltrate is the hallmark of acute cervicitis, caused by N. gonorrhoeae, HSV-2, or Chlamydia in its acute phase, but is not specific to any single organism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nabothian cysts with a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nabothian cysts are retention cysts of blocked endocervical glands, a classic finding in chronic cervicitis of any cause — not specific to Chlamydia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lymphoid follicles in the stroma (follicular cervicitis)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Follicular cervicitis — lymphoid follicles in the cervical stroma — is virtually the histological signature of Chlamydia trachomatis, which can silently infect the transformation zone and ascend to cause PID.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Granulomatous inflammation with Langhans giant cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Granulomatous cervicitis with Langhans giant cells suggests tuberculosis, not a sexually transmitted infection like Chlamydia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Follicular cervicitis — lymphoid follicles in the cervical stroma — is virtually pathognomonic for Chlamydia trachomatis. The infection is often silent but can ascend via the fallopian tubes to cause PID and infertility."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Follicular cervicitis is Chlamydia's histological fingerprint. Remember that Chlamydia infection in the transformation zone is often clinically silent but can ascend to cause tubal infertility."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
