          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which molecular alteration is the earliest event in the progression from atypica</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which molecular alteration is the earliest event in the progression from atypical endometrial hyperplasia to Type I endometrioid carcinoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>TP53 mutation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>TP53 mutation drives Type II (serous) endometrial carcinoma, which arises in atrophic endometrium — a completely different molecular pathway from Type I.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>KRAS mutation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>KRAS mutation is part of the Type I molecular signature but occurs later in the progression sequence, after the gatekeeper event has already initiated the pathway.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>BRCA1 loss</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>BRCA1 loss is a key risk driver for high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma, not for the endometrial hyperplasia-to-carcinoma sequence.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>PTEN tumour suppressor loss</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. PTEN loss is detected in over 80% of atypical hyperplasia cases and is the earliest molecular event in the Type I endometrioid carcinoma pathway.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "PTEN tumour suppressor loss is the earliest molecular event, found in >80% of atypical hyperplasia cases. Remember: TP53 mutation drives the separate Type II (serous) carcinoma pathway."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. PTEN loss is the gatekeeper event, seen in >80% of atypical hyperplasia cases, followed by microsatellite instability and KRAS mutation in the progression to Type I carcinoma."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
