          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 20-year-old woman presents with a unilateral ovarian mass. Her serum alpha-fet</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 20-year-old woman presents with a unilateral ovarian mass. Her serum alpha-fetoprotein is markedly elevated. Which histological finding would confirm the diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Schiller-Duval bodies</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Schiller-Duval bodies — glomeruloid structures around a central vessel — are pathognomonic for the yolk sac tumour, which produces alpha-fetoprotein.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Call-Exner bodies</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Call-Exner bodies are the signature of a granulosa cell tumour (sex cord-stromal category), which secretes oestrogen and inhibin — not alpha-fetoprotein.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Psammoma bodies</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Psammoma bodies are found in high-grade serous carcinoma, a surface epithelial tumour that typically affects older women and is not associated with AFP elevation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Signet-ring cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Signet-ring cells indicate a Krukenberg tumour — metastatic disease classically from a gastric primary — which typically presents as bilateral masses, not unilateral.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A young woman with a unilateral mass and markedly elevated alpha-fetoprotein has a yolk sac tumour. Its hallmark is the Schiller-Duval body — a glomeruloid structure around a central vessel."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Yolk sac tumour + elevated AFP + Schiller-Duval bodies is a classic diagnostic triad. In young women with an ovarian mass, always consider germ cell tumours first."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
