          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A hysterectomy specimen shows crowded back-to-back glands with enlarged nuclei a</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A hysterectomy specimen shows crowded back-to-back glands with enlarged nuclei and prominent nucleoli in one section. An adjacent section reveals glandular infiltration into the myometrium with a desmoplastic stromal response. What is the correct interpretation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Two separate pathologies: endometrial hyperplasia and adenomyosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adenomyosis places endometrial glands within the myometrium, but without desmoplastic stroma or nuclear atypia. A desmoplastic response signifies true invasion — this is carcinoma, not adenomyosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Atypical hyperplasia coexisting with invasive endometrioid carcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. This co-occurrence is found in 25–40% of hysterectomy specimens. The back-to-back atypical glands represent the precursor, while desmoplastic stroma with myometrial infiltration declares invasive carcinoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic endometritis with reactive glandular changes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic endometritis shows plasma cells in the stroma, not crowded atypical glands with loss of intervening stroma. The features here — nuclear atypia, back-to-back architecture, and myometrial invasion — indicate neoplasia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Endometrial polyp with unusual glandular architecture</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Endometrial polyps may show crowded glands but lack the severe nuclear atypia, stromal loss, and myometrial invasion with desmoplasia described here. This pattern indicates the hyperplasia-to-carcinoma sequence.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The combination of back-to-back atypical glands (atypical hyperplasia) alongside myometrial invasion with desmoplasia (invasive carcinoma) occurs in 25–40% of hysterectomy specimens. Desmoplastic stroma distinguishes true invasion from adenomyosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. This co-occurrence of precursor and invasive disease is a key recognition pattern — always examine the entire endometrium thoroughly when atypical glands are identified."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
